The thesis result tables in this directory, parity/golden/, and in its
subdirectories are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International licence (CC-BY-4.0), by decision of their author, Marc
Prinzing, on 2026-10-27. They were computed from per-asset losses derived
from the IRIS event set, and IRIS-derived data is treated as CC BY 4.0
(docs/data-sources.md, IRIS licence check). This decision supersedes the
CC0-1.0 dedication these files carried earlier; see
docs/adr/0001-name-and-licence.md, amendment 1.

Legal code: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
Summary: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Files covered:

- full_optimization_results.csv.gz, benchmarking_kpis.csv.gz: the thesis
  optimisation results and KPI table (gzipped copies of the thesis files);
- thesis_cluster_assignments.json, noise_digests.json: derived from the
  thesis code;
- golden_objectives.csv, golden_objectives.json: the objective values
  derived from the thesis tables and the thesis code;
- simulation_sweeps/: the thesis noise-level and asset-count sweep results,
  regenerated by running the thesis code.

Not covered:

- the parity fixture, parity/tests/data/thesis_eventset/, which is derived
  from the IRIS event set and records its own licence, CC-BY-4.0, in its
  metadata.json (docs/data-sources.md);
- the code under parity/, which is BSD-3-Clause (parity/LICENSE).

Attribution. When you share these tables or work based on them, give
credit, link to the licence, and indicate changes. Please credit:

- the tables: Marc Prinzing, parametric-insurance-design, parity/golden/,
  https://github.com/marc272/parametric-insurance-design, CC BY 4.0;
- the thesis code that produced them:
  https://github.com/marc272/parametric-insurance-framework;
- the hazard data they were computed from: Sparks, N. and Toumi, R. (2024).
  The Imperial College Storm Model (IRIS) Dataset. Scientific Data 11, 424,
  https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03250-y; dataset:
  https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.c.6724251.v1 (CC BY 4.0).

Changes from the IRIS data: per-asset losses for nine Miami assets were
computed with CLIMADA from the IRIS tracks, and the thesis optimisation and
simulation code was run on them to produce these tables.
